                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g_1,g_2\in C(X,Y)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝒮
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{S}
            in the sense that
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ∀
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi a
                  %mo :
                  %mi A
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ,
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo ∀
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi θ
                  %mo :
                  %mi Y
                  %mo →
                  %msub
                    %mi S
                    %mi a
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ,
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mi θ
                  %mo ∘
                  %msub
                    %mi g
                    %mn 1
                  %mo =
                  %mi θ
                  %mo ∘
                  %msub
                    %mi g
                    %mn 2
                  %mo ,
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \forall (a: A),\; \forall (\theta: Y \to S_a),\; \theta \circ g_1 = \theta \circ g_2 ,
          %p
            then
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi g
                      %mn 1
                    %mo =
                    %msub
                      %mi g
                      %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g_1 = g_2
          %h2#examples Examples
          %p
            In
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Set"} Set
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/truth+value"} truth value
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/well-pointed+topos"} well-pointed topos
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/subobject+classifier"} subobject classifier
            is a cogenerator.
          %p Much more generally, we have
          .num_prop
            %h6#proposition Proposition
            %p
              Every
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topos"} topos
              with a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/size+issues"} small set
              of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/separator"} generators
              (e.g., a well-pointed topos, or a Grothendieck topos), and that has
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/products"} products
              of objects indexed over sets no larger in
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cardinality"} cardinality
              than the generating set, admits an
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/injective+object"} injective
              cogenerator.
          .proof
            %h6#proof Proof
            %p
              Let
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
              be a set of generators for the topos; as usual, let
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Ω
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Omega
              be the subobject classifier. We claim that a product
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %munder
                      %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∏
                      %mrow
                        %mi c
                        %mo ∈
                        %mi C
                    %msup
                      %mi Ω
                      %mi c
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \prod_{c \in C} \Omega^c
            %p
              is a cogenerator. For suppose
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mo ,
                    %mi g
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mi X
                    %mover
                      %mo →
                      %mo →
                    %mi Y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f, g \colon X \stackrel{\to}{\to} Y
              are distinct morphisms. The contravariant power object functor
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi Ω
                      %mo −
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Omega^-
              is faithful (a familiar fact, since it is
              = succeed ")," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monadic+functor"} monadic
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi Ω
                      %mi f
                    %mo ,
                    %msup
                      %mi Ω
                      %mi g
                    %mo :
                    %msup
                      %mi Ω
                      %mi Y
                    %mover
                      %mo →
                      %mo →
                    %msup
                      %mi Ω
                      %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Omega^f, \Omega^g: \Omega^Y \stackrel{\to}{\to} \Omega^X
              are distinct. Since the objects
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi c
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} c
              form a
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/separator"} generating set
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi h
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mi c
                    %mo →
                    %msup
                      %mi Ω
                      %mi Y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} h \colon c \to \Omega^Y
              such that the composites
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi c
                    %mover
                      %mo →
                      %mi h
                    %msup
                      %mi Ω
                      %mi Y
                    %mover
                      %munder
                        %mo →
                        %mrow
                          %msup
                            %mi Ω
                            %mi g
                      %mover
                        %mo →
                        %mrow
                          %msup
                            %mi Ω
                            %mi f
                    %msup
                      %mi Ω
                      %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} c \stackrel{h}{\to} \Omega^Y \stackrel{\overset{\Omega^f}{\to}}{\underset{\Omega^g}{\to}} \Omega^X
            %p
              are distinct. The map
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi h
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} h
              may be transformed to a map
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mover
                        %mi h
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ˜
                      %mo :
                      %mi Y
                      %mo →
                      %msup
                        %mi Ω
                        %mi c
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \tilde{h}: Y \to \Omega^c
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                    %mover
                      %munder
                        %mo →
                        %mi g
                      %mover
                        %mo →
                        %mi f
                    %mi Y
                    %mover
                      %mo →
                      %mover
                        %mi h
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ˜
                    %msup
                      %mi Ω
                      %mi c
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X \stackrel{\overset{f}{\to}}{\underset{g}{\to}} Y \stackrel{\tilde{h}}{\to} \Omega^c
            %p
              are distinct. For any other
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi c
                      %mo ′
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi C
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} c' \in C
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Y
                    %mo →
                    %msup
                      %mi Ω
                      %mrow
                        %mi c
                        %mo ′
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y \to \Omega^{c'}
              to be the map classifying the maximal
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/subobject"} subobject
              of
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi c
                      %mo ′
                      %mo ×
                      %mi Y
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} c' \times Y
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mover
                      %mi h
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ˜
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \tilde{h}
              collectively induce a map
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Y
                    %mo →
                    %munder
                      %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∏
                      %mrow
                        %mi c
                        %mo ∈
                        %mi C
                    %msup
                      %mi Ω
                      %mi c
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y \to \prod_{c \in C} \Omega^c
            %p
              that yields distinct results when composed with
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
              and
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi g
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g
            %p
              The object
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∏
                    %msup
                      %mi Ω
                      %mi c
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \prod \Omega^c
              is injective because already
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Ω
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Omega
              is injective (see Mac Lane-Moerdijk, IV.10), and it is a general fact that in a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cartesian+closed+category"} cartesian closed category
              (or more generally a
              = succeed ")," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/closed+monoidal+category"} closed monoidal category
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi X
                      %mi Y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X^Y
              whose base
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              is injective is also injective, and products of injective objects are injective.
          %p
            Notice also that the existence of a small (co)generating family is one of the conditions in one version of the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/adjoint+functor+theorem"} adjoint functor theorem
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Grothendieck+toposes"} Grothendieck toposes
            are cototal (q.v.).
          %h3#in_topology In topology
          %p The existence of cogenerators is useful to produce adjoint functor theorems for certain categories of topological spaces, as well, as these are usually not locally presentable like most other typical “large” categories of mathematics.
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                In the category of all topological spaces, a single space
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
                is a cogenerator if and only if it is not
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi T
                        %mn 0
                      %mo ,
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T_0,
                since
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi T
                        %mn 0
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T_0
                spaces can’t distinguish the points of a
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi T
                        %mn 0
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T_0
                space and, conversely, any such space has the two-point indiscrete space, which cogenerates for the same reason as a two-point set, as a retract.
            %li
              %p
                In compact Hausdorff spaces, which are algebraic but not locally presentable, the interval
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                      %mn 0
                      %mo ,
                      %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [0,1]
                is a cogenerator, as follows essentially from Gelfand duality.
            %li
              %p
                Many other categories of spaces, but not all, have cogenerators; for more see the answers and references
                = succeed "." do
                  %a{:href => "https://ncatlab.org/nlab/edit/cogenerator"} here
          %h2#terminology Terminology
          %p
            The concept of
            %em cogenerator
            is dual to that of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/separator"} separator
            = succeed "." do
              %em coseparator
          %h2#related_concepts Related concepts
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/generator"} generator
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/separator"} separator
            %li
              %p
                %strong cogenerator
